// The typefaces the interface is set in.
//
// Globality Atlas typography: a restrained interface face for data, and a serif for the
// things that are read rather than scanned - screen titles, document heads, country names.
//
// Why two faces and not one. A grand strategy interface is a document and a ledger at the
// same time. The ledger wants a grotesque with flat sides and tabular figures; the document
// wants a serif, because a serif is what a printed atlas uses for a heading and the eye reads
// it as "this is the title" before it reads a single letter. With one face for both, every
// heading looked like a bigger caption, which is the whole reason the interface read as flat.
//
// Nothing new is bundled: these are system families with fallbacks, and the engine's own face
// is the last resort, exactly as before. A mod may ship its licensed files and use the `file`
// role instead of `system_families`.
//
// Roles:
//   ui.default   - buttons, rows, tables, body text
//   ui.display   - screen titles, document heads, the big figure over a section
//   map.countries, map.cities - the globe's own lettering
{
  "fonts": {
    "ui.default": { "system_families": ["Segoe UI Variable Text", "Segoe UI", "Noto Sans", "DejaVu Sans", "Arial"] },
    "ui.display": { "system_families": ["Georgia", "Noto Serif", "Liberation Serif", "DejaVu Serif"] },
    "map.countries": { "system_families": ["Georgia", "Noto Serif", "Liberation Serif"] },
    "map.cities": { "system_families": ["Georgia", "Noto Serif", "Liberation Serif"] }
  }
}
